{
  "candidate_0005": {
    "reason": "The assertion that a causal attention mask is constructed as a binary matrix where entries for later positions are set to zero is supported by general knowledge of decoder-only transformer architectures (text_1). This construction enforces the autoregressive constraint by preventing a token at position $i$ from attending to tokens at positions $j > i$. The qualification 'where entries for later positions are set to zero' accurately describes the mechanism without contradicting the source text or introducing unsupported claims. The relation 'is_constructed_as' correctly links the functional concept (mask) to its data structure implementation.",
    "candidate_id": "candidate_0005",
    "position": "defend"
  },
  "candidate_0009": {
    "reason": "The assertion that a causal attention mask restricts the attention matrix to be 'lower-triangular' is technically incorrect and must be withdrawn. In a causal setup, preventing attention to later positions ($j > i$) requires setting those specific entries to zero. This results in a valid attention region corresponding to the lower triangle (including the diagonal), but the *mask* itself (the restriction) is an upper-triangular zero-mask. Asserting the matrix is restricted 'to be lower-triangular' implies the constraint applies to the lower triangle or misidentifies the shape of the invalid region. The objection correctly identifies this geometric error: allowing attention to later positions would violate causality. Therefore, the claim contradicts standard definitions and the functional requirement of preventing future-looking attention.",
    "candidate_id": "candidate_0009",
    "position": "withdraw"
  }
}
